  "final": "The proof's valid core is the identification of the winning set $W$ as triangles containing an angle from $S = \\{k\\theta \\mid k \\in \\mathbb{Z}, 0 < k\\theta < 180^\\circ\\}$ and the inductive argument that any triangle with an angle in $S$ can be reduced to a triangle with an angle $\\theta$.\n\nThe earliest reproducible gap occurs in the sufficiency proof for $n \\ge 3$. The claim \"Such a $k$ always exists because the interval $(C, C+A)$ has length $A > \\theta$\" is false for the domain of triangles where the largest angle $C$ satisfies $C > 180^\\circ - \\theta$. In such cases, the interval $(C, C+A)$ extends beyond $180^\\circ$, and since any angle $k\\theta$ must be strictly less than $180^\\circ$, there may be no integer $k$ such that $C < k\\theta < 180^\\circ$. For example, if $\\theta = 60^\\circ$ and $C = 170^\\circ$, the only multiples of $\\theta$ are $60^\\circ$ and $120^\\circ$, neither of which lies in the interval $(170^\\circ, 170^\\circ + A)$.\n\nMaterial reviewer errors discarded:\n1. OPC's claim that the definition of $W$ is flawed: The recursive definition is standard for game-theoretic winning positions.\n2. OPC's claim that the necessity proof is incorrect: The logic that $180^\\circ/\\theta \\notin \\mathbb{Z}$ prevents Mulan from forcing both children into $W_S$ is sound.\n\nThe retained issue is fatal because it invalidates the sufficiency claim for a large class of triangles (those with an angle $> 180^\\circ - \\theta$). The proof fails to show that Mulan can guarantee a win for these cases, and the specific construction provided is inapplicable. This is a fallacy/incomplete argument requiring a different cutting strategy (e.g., splitting the largest angle) to resolve.\n\nActionable repair guidance: The sufficiency proof must be extended to cover triangles where the largest angle $C > 180^\\circ - \\theta$. Instead of cutting from the vertex of the smallest angle, Mulan should cut the largest angle $C$ into $\\theta$ and